// Pull request author: saakovv
// Change: new rule
// Title: Linux Privilege Escalation via GDB Debugger
// Sigma ID: f9b5a1d8-2c6e-4f21-a8b9-1d7c3e6f5a90
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ivan Saakov
// Date: 2026-05-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, T1548.003
// Description: Detects use of the GDB debugger to attach to running processes or execute shell commands, which
//   can be leveraged for privilege escalation. When executed with sudo or against privileged
//   processes via --pid, GDB can inject and execute arbitrary code in the context of that process.
//   This technique allows attackers to spawn root shells by calling system() or execve() within a
//   privileged process without writing files to disk.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate developer debugging of processes in a development environment
//   - Security researchers analyzing binaries in an isolated sandbox
// References:
//   - https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/gdb/
//   - https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/gdb.1.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e5bac28da3655c33390db7ef1503db8cd3babb5f/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_gdb_privilege_escalation.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "/gdb" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "--pid" or ProcessCommandLine contains " -p " or ProcessCommandLine contains "--eval-command" or ProcessCommandLine contains "-ex " or ProcessCommandLine contains "call system" or ProcessCommandLine contains "call execve")) or (ProcessCommandLine contains "sudo" and ProcessCommandLine contains "gdb")
